WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:02.534
-Come on, buddy.
You look fantastic.

00:00:02.534 --> 00:00:04.234
It is great to see you.
-I know. I can't help it.

00:00:04.234 --> 00:00:07.968
-No. Oh, my -- I want to talk.
I have so many questions.

00:00:07.968 --> 00:00:11.234
First of all, the first album
in almost 20 years.

00:00:11.234 --> 00:00:13.067
-Almost.
-Yeah, almost. Yeah.

00:00:13.067 --> 00:00:15.367
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:15.367 --> 00:00:17.000
-Give me a break.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:19.267
-How does it feel
to perform the album live?

00:00:19.267 --> 00:00:21.601
I saw you last night.
Here's a picture of you.

00:00:21.601 --> 00:00:24.467
-Oh, last night
was the first time

00:00:24.467 --> 00:00:28.334
that we performed new songs

00:00:28.334 --> 00:00:31.434
with the exception
of one or two things,

00:00:31.434 --> 00:00:33.667
like "Doom and Gloom."
-Yeah.

00:00:33.667 --> 00:00:37.534
-But a great feeling.

00:00:37.534 --> 00:00:40.434
You know, the band feels
really good about it.

00:00:40.434 --> 00:00:43.968
And a band exists...

00:00:43.968 --> 00:00:45.367
am I right, gentlemen --

00:00:45.367 --> 00:00:48.033
...on what it does.
[ Laughs ]

00:00:48.033 --> 00:00:50.467
-Yeah. And --

00:00:50.467 --> 00:00:53.267
-Yeah. "A band exists
on what it does."

00:00:53.267 --> 00:00:54.968
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:54.968 --> 00:00:58.534
I love that. "A band exists
on what it does."

00:00:58.534 --> 00:01:01.367
These are gems. These are
little Hackney Diamonds

00:01:01.367 --> 00:01:04.067
he's gonna drop
all through the interviews.

00:01:04.067 --> 00:01:08.000
You played it --
And we were talking --

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:09.767
I got to see you in London.

00:01:09.767 --> 00:01:11.467
And we were talking about
these songs, and you go,

00:01:11.467 --> 00:01:12.968
"Well, I think these are good,"
and you go,

00:01:12.968 --> 00:01:15.000
"I think they're gonna be good
playing them live."

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:16.467
And I go, "Yeah."

00:01:16.467 --> 00:01:19.234
And then you play
this little club last night.

00:01:19.234 --> 00:01:21.934
Why didn't you play the --
You surprised everybody.

00:01:21.934 --> 00:01:23.667
But why the little club?

00:01:23.667 --> 00:01:25.634
-Listen. Bands like us...

00:01:25.634 --> 00:01:28.033
And, matter of fact,
all bands and groups,

00:01:28.033 --> 00:01:31.634
eventually --
they start in little rooms

00:01:31.634 --> 00:01:34.267
playing to a very few people.
-Yeah.

00:01:34.267 --> 00:01:37.501
-And -- And that's where
you hone down your stuff

00:01:37.501 --> 00:01:39.400
and that's where you --
you got to --

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:43.033
If it gets bigger, good luck.
You know? I mean...

00:01:43.033 --> 00:01:44.200
-That's great for you.

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:45.968
But it doesn't necessarily
always get --

00:01:45.968 --> 00:01:49.300
-Yeah, but, I mean,
the essence of a band

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:52.501
is in a small room

00:01:52.501 --> 00:01:55.334
with a lot of people,
if you're lucky.

00:01:55.334 --> 00:01:57.467
-Yeah.

00:01:57.467 --> 00:02:02.100
-That's where you work --
work your stuff out.

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:03.834
-You came out, you had -- Look.

00:02:03.834 --> 00:02:05.767
I mean, this is a photo
from last night.

00:02:05.767 --> 00:02:07.634
There's no tricks.
There's no anything.

00:02:07.634 --> 00:02:12.033
It's you and a guitar and drums
and a microphone, and that's it.

00:02:12.033 --> 00:02:13.734
And you're like,
"Yeah, put me in the ring.

00:02:13.734 --> 00:02:15.334
Let me show you how...
I'll show you...

00:02:15.334 --> 00:02:17.033
Come on.
I'll show you how to do it.

00:02:17.033 --> 00:02:19.634
Let's go! Let's go!"

00:02:19.634 --> 00:02:23.534
Loved it! It was so great!
It was so cool.

00:02:23.534 --> 00:02:25.767
Did you notice me there
in the audience?

00:02:25.767 --> 00:02:27.267
-I saw you in the back there.

00:02:27.267 --> 00:02:28.968
-Yeah, I wasn't in the back.
I was...

00:02:28.968 --> 00:02:31.567
[ Laughter ]

00:02:31.567 --> 00:02:32.868
But they're great songs.

00:02:32.868 --> 00:02:35.734
And now we can talk about
who's on the album.

00:02:35.734 --> 00:02:37.868
Lady Gaga was off the chain.

00:02:37.868 --> 00:02:39.501
She's on the album.
She was fantastic last night.

00:02:39.501 --> 00:02:41.100
-She is on the album.

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:43.434
She insisted on being part
of last night.

00:02:43.434 --> 00:02:45.667
And bless her heart.
You know, bless her.

00:02:45.667 --> 00:02:47.767
-She crushed it, but also --
Can we talk about this?

00:02:47.767 --> 00:02:49.834
This "Bite My Head Off,"
which I think is one of

00:02:49.834 --> 00:02:52.300
my favorite songs on the album,
has a bass player.

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:56.133
-That is understandable.
-Thank you. Yes.

00:02:56.133 --> 00:02:58.100
Paul McCartney plays

00:02:58.100 --> 00:03:00.100
with the Rolling Stones
on this album.

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:02.701
A Beatle. A Beatle.

00:03:02.701 --> 00:03:04.734
A Beatle and the Rolling Stones

00:03:04.734 --> 00:03:08.534
played on one song together
for the first time.

00:03:08.534 --> 00:03:11.000
-No.
-What?

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:12.100
What are you -- What?

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:13.567
-John Lennon and Paul.

00:03:13.567 --> 00:03:18.234
First off, they gave us
one of our first big hits,

00:03:18.234 --> 00:03:19.834
"I Wanna Be Your Man."

00:03:19.834 --> 00:03:23.300
They were the ones
that collared us and said,

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:25.467
"Hey, we've got this song here,
you know?"

00:03:25.467 --> 00:03:29.334
-That's right.
-And so we -- Our relationship

00:03:29.334 --> 00:03:32.000
with John and Paul
and with the Beatles was --

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:36.167
It went -- It goes back
to '62, '63, you know, and...

00:03:36.167 --> 00:03:38.033
-What was the relationship?

00:03:38.033 --> 00:03:39.434
Did you hate each other?
Did you...?

00:03:39.434 --> 00:03:42.667
-No. We'd play up to that.

00:03:42.667 --> 00:03:44.467
I think, you know,
there was, like,

00:03:44.467 --> 00:03:47.234
a "Beatles versus Stones" thing
and that.

00:03:47.234 --> 00:03:50.133
Oh yeah, yeah, but not really.
-No.

00:03:50.133 --> 00:03:52.200
-Meanwhile we're
working together saying,

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:54.601
"Is your single ready?"

00:03:54.601 --> 00:03:56.534
-Yeah, yeah.
-And...

00:03:56.534 --> 00:03:57.767
-"I'll put ours out."

00:03:57.767 --> 00:03:59.567
-"We have a couple
of weeks to go."

00:03:59.567 --> 00:04:01.501
"Okay. You go first."

00:04:01.501 --> 00:04:05.868
So we tried not to clash
with our stuff.

00:04:05.868 --> 00:04:07.567
They gave us songs.

00:04:07.567 --> 00:04:12.300
Also, Paul and John are on
a track called "We Love You"...

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:14.567
-Oh, yeah!
-...in the middle '60s.

00:04:14.567 --> 00:04:17.801
And another track, "Dandelion,"

00:04:17.801 --> 00:04:21.067
that John and Paul
came by to sing, you know.

00:04:21.067 --> 00:04:23.234
Because they liked
the Stones too, you know.

00:04:23.234 --> 00:04:26.467
-Yeah. So...
That was awesome. Yeah.

00:04:26.467 --> 00:04:28.334
You inspired each other.
-Yeah.

00:04:28.334 --> 00:04:30.767
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:30.767 --> 00:04:34.000
-And we don't
have to get too into it

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:35.767
if it makes you sad at all,

00:04:35.767 --> 00:04:38.601
but I know your brother
Charlie Watts passed away.

00:04:38.601 --> 00:04:41.100
And it was very sad.
We all loved Charlie Watts.

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:42.400
-It is, of course.

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:45.033
-But he got to play drums
on two of these tracks.

00:04:45.033 --> 00:04:48.000
And the rest were played --
Steve Jordan, who's phenomenal.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:51.934
-Steve, who was recommended
by Mr. Watts.

00:04:51.934 --> 00:04:53.767
-Yes.
-Which is, you know --

00:04:53.767 --> 00:04:57.634
That's the best recommendation
you can get, you know?

00:04:57.634 --> 00:04:59.133
-Yeah. Exactly.

00:04:59.133 --> 00:05:00.934
-And also
they were great friends.

00:05:00.934 --> 00:05:03.367
And it was --
Charlie had always said to me,

00:05:03.367 --> 00:05:06.934
"If you're gonna do anything
outside of the Stones,

00:05:06.934 --> 00:05:08.367
do it with Steve Jordan."

00:05:08.367 --> 00:05:10.701
And now it's turned out
that, you know,

00:05:10.701 --> 00:05:12.901
Steve's sitting
in Charlie's chair.

00:05:12.901 --> 00:05:15.100
With great reverence.

00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:17.968
And we do miss the man so much.

00:05:17.968 --> 00:05:19.601
But, I mean,
if there was another guy

00:05:19.601 --> 00:05:22.801
that was gonna fill
that seat, it's Steve, right?

00:05:22.801 --> 00:05:25.200
Am I right?
-Yeah, yeah. And Steve --

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:27.434
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:27.434 --> 00:05:29.133
-Uh...

00:05:29.133 --> 00:05:31.033
I'm gonna ask you
some questions

00:05:31.033 --> 00:05:32.801
that everyone's probably
asked you a million times.

00:05:32.801 --> 00:05:34.167
You already told the answer.

00:05:34.167 --> 00:05:38.901
But the name "Rolling Stones"
is from Muddy Waters?

00:05:38.901 --> 00:05:40.501
-Rolling Stones. Yeah.

00:05:40.501 --> 00:05:44.100
We come -- Our name
comes from Muddy Waters.

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:48.033
You know, we come from
a long line of blues players.

00:05:48.033 --> 00:05:52.534
And the only reason we are
called the Rolling Stones

00:05:52.534 --> 00:05:56.100
is that we were
making a phone call.

00:05:56.100 --> 00:06:00.734
We had a gig. This was way,
way back, you know?

00:06:00.734 --> 00:06:02.133
-Yeah.

00:06:02.133 --> 00:06:04.801
-And we finally got a gig

00:06:04.801 --> 00:06:07.767
and we said,
"Let's put an ad out," you know?

00:06:07.767 --> 00:06:11.167
And I think it was Brian Jones
at the time.

00:06:11.167 --> 00:06:13.200
Called "Jazz News" or something.

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:18.300
"We're doing a gig
at the, you know, Windmill Pub,"

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:19.801
blah, blah, blah.

00:06:19.801 --> 00:06:21.968
And they said,
"That's all very well,

00:06:21.968 --> 00:06:23.701
but what are you called?"

00:06:23.701 --> 00:06:25.033
[ Laughs ] You know?

00:06:25.033 --> 00:06:28.601
And down there on the floor
was Muddy Waters' album.

00:06:28.601 --> 00:06:32.400
And the first track
was "Rollin' Stone Blues."

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:34.734
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:34.734 --> 00:06:36.200
There it is.

00:06:36.200 --> 00:06:39.567
[ Laughing ]

00:06:39.567 --> 00:06:42.400
-You've been a great friend
to our show

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:44.133
and a great friend to me.
Thank you very much.

00:06:44.133 --> 00:06:45.801
I don't know if you remember
the first time

00:06:45.801 --> 00:06:47.868
you came on this program.

00:06:47.868 --> 00:06:51.701
You were -- You were smoking.
-Oh.

00:06:51.701 --> 00:06:55.000
-Yeah. In the --
In the dressing room.

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:58.067
Yeah, and we --
And the fire alarms went off.

00:06:58.067 --> 00:06:59.868
[ Laughter ]
This is a true story.

00:06:59.868 --> 00:07:02.367
And these blinking lights
are all in the hallway,

00:07:02.367 --> 00:07:05.100
and I've never -- I didn't know
that this was possible,

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:07.767
but two fire marshals
came down.

00:07:07.767 --> 00:07:09.968
-True.
-I know that they exist,

00:07:09.968 --> 00:07:11.534
but I'd never seen them.

00:07:11.534 --> 00:07:14.634
And it's alarms going off.
"Woop woop woop woop woop!"

00:07:14.634 --> 00:07:16.100
And they go into
your dressing room

00:07:16.100 --> 00:07:17.868
and you're sitting there
and you're smoking.

00:07:17.868 --> 00:07:19.367
You had a --
You had an ashtray

00:07:19.367 --> 00:07:22.100
that apparently
would suck the smoke out.

00:07:22.100 --> 00:07:24.701
Didn't work, by the way.
And --

00:07:24.701 --> 00:07:27.667
-I didn't know it was a sin.
-Yeah.

00:07:27.667 --> 00:07:31.567
-The guy came in, and he goes,
"Mr. Richards, you can't smoke."

00:07:31.567 --> 00:07:34.033
And you go, "Yeah, I know.
It's not good for your health."

00:07:34.033 --> 00:07:36.067
[ Laughter ]

00:07:36.067 --> 00:07:38.067
[ Applause ]

00:07:38.067 --> 00:07:39.868
Swear to God.
-Yeah.

00:07:39.868 --> 00:07:41.901
-I swear. I watched it happen.

00:07:41.901 --> 00:07:45.400
But we got talking.
We got talking about something.

00:07:45.400 --> 00:07:47.133
And I said --
I saw you do an interview

00:07:47.133 --> 00:07:48.868
where you're walking
around your house,

00:07:48.868 --> 00:07:51.834
and you have a lemon tree.
-Oh, that. Yeah.

00:07:51.834 --> 00:07:54.767
-Yeah, and I go --
-It's vanished by now.

00:07:54.767 --> 00:07:57.367
-Yes, of course, but I go,
"Talk to me about your lemons."

00:07:57.367 --> 00:07:59.701
You know, I'm fascinated
that you have a lemon tree

00:07:59.701 --> 00:08:01.734
and you pick a lemon
and you put it in a drink

00:08:01.734 --> 00:08:03.534
and you make lemonade
and you do this, all this stuff.

00:08:03.534 --> 00:08:05.167
And you -- We were laughing,

00:08:05.167 --> 00:08:08.067
and then we went to a party,
a mutual friend.

00:08:08.067 --> 00:08:10.300
And you come in. I go,
"Keith, what's up? It's Jimmy.

00:08:10.300 --> 00:08:12.067
How are you doing, man?"
I don't even know
if you remember me.

00:08:12.067 --> 00:08:14.400
And you go, "Hey, Jimmy.
How you doing, buddy?
I know it's your birthday."

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:17.267
And you pulled out
of your pocket a lemon.

00:08:17.267 --> 00:08:18.934
-I did.

00:08:18.934 --> 00:08:21.367
-And you gave me a lemon.

00:08:21.367 --> 00:08:23.300
And you said --
You wrote "Happy Birthday."

00:08:23.300 --> 00:08:25.801
-I forgot the show-business
aspect of this.

00:08:25.801 --> 00:08:27.634
-No. Yeah. Yeah. There's a
show-business story to it.

00:08:27.634 --> 00:08:30.367
And you gave me this lemon,
and I loved it so much.

00:08:30.367 --> 00:08:31.934
And I just want to tell you,

00:08:31.934 --> 00:08:35.000
I took the lemon
and I opened it up

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:37.167
and I took the seed
out of the lemon,

00:08:37.167 --> 00:08:39.701
and I put it in a Ziploc bag
with a paper towel.

00:08:39.701 --> 00:08:41.200
I'm serious about this.

00:08:41.200 --> 00:08:44.634
And I grew a lemon tree
from your lemon.

00:08:44.634 --> 00:08:46.033
[ Applause ]

00:08:46.033 --> 00:08:47.701
-This is a new one on me.

00:08:47.701 --> 00:08:49.467
-No, this is for real.

00:08:49.467 --> 00:08:51.467
-Ohh!

00:08:51.467 --> 00:08:54.834
-That's -- That's your tree.

00:08:54.834 --> 00:08:56.300
[ Laughter ]

00:08:56.300 --> 00:08:59.634
That's your tree, bud.
It's 10 years old.

00:08:59.634 --> 00:09:02.434
Happy birthday, lemon tree!

00:09:02.434 --> 00:09:04.801
It's mine. I'm keeping it.
-You're keeping it.

00:09:04.801 --> 00:09:07.434
-More with Keith Richards
when we come back, everybody,

00:09:07.434 --> 00:09:09.634
This is -- This is your lemon.

